{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"voronoi"},{"type":"text","value":" partitions the final plot into the cells nearest to each unique\nscaled x/y position. It emits renderer-neutral polygons clipped to the final\nplot bounds. Import it from the optional spatial subpath; it is not included in\nthe root or universal barrel."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { voronoi } from '@tanstack/charts/spatial/voronoi'\n\nconst marks = [\n  voronoi(cars, {\n    x: 'weight',\n    y: 'economy',\n    key: 'id',\n    color: 'cylinders',\n    fillOpacity: 0.14,\n    stroke: '#fff',\n    strokeWidth: 1,\n  }),\n  dot(cars, {\n    x: 'weight',\n    y: 'economy',\n    key: 'id',\n    color: 'cylinders',\n  }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function voronoi<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: VoronoiOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<never, never, never, InferredX, InferredY>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mark contributes x/y and color domains but intentionally emits no\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":" interaction candidates."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source horizontal position"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source vertical position"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One tessellation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independent full-plot topology group"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inferred"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable source identity and coincident-cell ownership"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value sent to the color scale; never a topology group"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<TDatum, string>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell fill"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<TDatum, string>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell stroke"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Renderer default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell presentation"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeDasharray"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell stroke dash pattern"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opacity"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Renderer default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whole-cell opacity"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer-derived"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable mark ID"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkMotionOptions<never>['motion']"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell enter, update, and exit motion"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"},{"type":"text","value":" accessors receive the original source row, source index,\nand complete source array."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"final-screen-topology","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final-screen topology"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete x/y pairs establish the positional domains. After scales and inner\nbounds resolve, the mark projects both axes, computes the Voronoi diagram in\nscreen space, and clips every cell to the plot rectangle. Resizing can change\ncell geometry even when source values and domains are unchanged."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inversion is not required, so continuous, temporal, and categorical\npositional scales are supported. Missing, nonfinite, or unmappable pairs are\nomitted without allowing one coordinate to expand the other coordinate's\ndomain. Zero valid positions produce no cells; one unique position fills the\nplot; two positions split it; and collinear positions remain valid."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The generated polygons are ordinary scene areas, not SVG path strings, so the\nsame definition works with SVG, Canvas, and static rendering."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"groups-and-identity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Groups and identity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" creates an independent full-plot tessellation for every group.\nThose diagrams overlap. Omit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" when all observations should share one\npartition; use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" alone to paint categories without changing topology."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" when source order can change. Exact coincident positions produce\none cell within each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" group, with ownership chosen from a canonical ordering\nof stable keys. This makes coincident ownership and cell keys deterministic\nacross reorderings. Distinct projected positions remain distinct even when\nthey are much closer than one pixel because nearby sites can still divide a\nlarge visible region. The mark fails instead of merging sites if separate cell\nboundaries cannot be represented by finite screen coordinates. Without an\nexplicit key, the mark tries a unique top-level or nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", then falls back\nto source index."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"visible-cells-versus-focus","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Visible cells versus focus"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Voronoi polygons are a visible encoding layer, not an interaction index. They\nare hidden from accessibility navigation and add no pointer or keyboard focus\ncandidates. Layer "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":" or another interactive mark over the cells when the\nsource observations should drive nearest-point focus and tooltips. Both layers\ncan share x, y, color, and key channels without application-owned scale\nprojection or tooltip state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If no cells should be painted and only lookup performance matters, provide a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpatialIndexFactory"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of adding a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"voronoi"},{"type":"text","value":" mark. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"options","text":"Options","level":2},{"id":"final-screen-topology","text":"Final-screen topology","level":2},{"id":"groups-and-identity","text":"Groups and identity","level":2},{"id":"visible-cells-versus-focus","text":"Visible cells versus focus","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n`voronoi` partitions the final plot into the cells nearest to each unique\nscaled x/y position. It emits renderer-neutral polygons clipped to the final\nplot bounds. Import it from the optional spatial subpath; it is not included in\nthe root or universal barrel.\n\n```ts\nimport { dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { voronoi } from '@tanstack/charts/spatial/voronoi'\n\nconst marks = [\n  voronoi(cars, {\n    x: 'weight',\n    y: 'economy',\n    key: 'id',\n    color: 'cylinders',\n    fillOpacity: 0.14,\n    stroke: '#fff',\n    strokeWidth: 1,\n  }),\n  dot(cars, {\n    x: 'weight',\n    y: 'economy',\n    key: 'id',\n    color: 'cylinders',\n  }),\n]\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction voronoi<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: VoronoiOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<never, never, never, InferredX, InferredY>\n```\n\nThe mark contributes x/y and color domains but intentionally emits no\n`ChartPoint` interaction candidates.\n\n## Options\n\n| Option                                            | Type                                      | Default          | Meaning                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `x`                                               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`            | Required         | Source horizontal position                            |\n| `y`                                               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`            | Required         | Source vertical position                              |\n| `z`                                               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`              | One tessellation | Independent full-plot topology group                  |\n| `key`                                             | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`               | Inferred         | Stable source identity and coincident-cell ownership  |\n| `color`                                           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`              | No value         | Value sent to the color scale; never a topology group |\n| `fill`                                            | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`           | Resolved color   | Cell fill                                             |\n| `stroke`                                          | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`           | None             | Cell stroke                                           |\n| `fillOpacity`, `strokeOpacity`, and `strokeWidth` | `number`                                  | Renderer default | Cell presentation                                     |\n| `strokeDasharray`                                 | `string`                                  | None             | Cell stroke dash pattern                              |\n| `opacity`                                         | `number`                                  | Renderer default | Whole-cell opacity                                    |\n| `id`                                              | `string`                                  | Layer-derived    | Stable mark ID                                        |\n| `motion`                                          | `ChartMarkMotionOptions<never>['motion']` | None             | Cell enter, update, and exit motion                   |\n\n`fill` and `stroke` accessors receive the original source row, source index,\nand complete source array.\n\n## Final-screen topology\n\nComplete x/y pairs establish the positional domains. After scales and inner\nbounds resolve, the mark projects both axes, computes the Voronoi diagram in\nscreen space, and clips every cell to the plot rectangle. Resizing can change\ncell geometry even when source values and domains are unchanged.\n\nInversion is not required, so continuous, temporal, and categorical\npositional scales are supported. Missing, nonfinite, or unmappable pairs are\nomitted without allowing one coordinate to expand the other coordinate's\ndomain. Zero valid positions produce no cells; one unique position fills the\nplot; two positions split it; and collinear positions remain valid.\n\nThe generated polygons are ordinary scene areas, not SVG path strings, so the\nsame definition works with SVG, Canvas, and static rendering.\n\n## Groups and identity\n\nAn explicit `z` creates an independent full-plot tessellation for every group.\nThose diagrams overlap. Omit `z` when all observations should share one\npartition; use `color` alone to paint categories without changing topology.\n\nSupply `key` when source order can change. Exact coincident positions produce\none cell within each `z` group, with ownership chosen from a canonical ordering\nof stable keys. This makes coincident ownership and cell keys deterministic\nacross reorderings. Distinct projected positions remain distinct even when\nthey are much closer than one pixel because nearby sites can still divide a\nlarge visible region. The mark fails instead of merging sites if separate cell\nboundaries cannot be represented by finite screen coordinates. Without an\nexplicit key, the mark tries a unique top-level or nested `id`, then falls back\nto source index.\n\n## Visible cells versus focus\n\nVoronoi polygons are a visible encoding layer, not an interaction index. They\nare hidden from accessibility navigation and add no pointer or keyboard focus\ncandidates. Layer `dot` or another interactive mark over the cells when the\nsource observations should drive nearest-point focus and tooltips. Both layers\ncan share x, y, color, and key channels without application-owned scale\nprojection or tooltip state.\n\nIf no cells should be painted and only lookup performance matters, provide a\n`ChartSpatialIndexFactory` instead of adding a `voronoi` mark. See\n[Tooltips and Focus](../../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Voronoi Mark\ndescription: Reference for responsive final-screen Voronoi cells, grouping, stable coincident ownership, presentation, and interaction separation.\n---\n\n`voronoi` partitions the final plot into the cells nearest to each unique\nscaled x/y position. It emits renderer-neutral polygons clipped to the final\nplot bounds. Import it from the optional spatial subpath; it is not included in\nthe root or universal barrel.\n\n```ts\nimport { dot } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { voronoi } from '@tanstack/charts/spatial/voronoi'\n\nconst marks = [\n  voronoi(cars, {\n    x: 'weight',\n    y: 'economy',\n    key: 'id',\n    color: 'cylinders',\n    fillOpacity: 0.14,\n    stroke: '#fff',\n    strokeWidth: 1,\n  }),\n  dot(cars, {\n    x: 'weight',\n    y: 'economy',\n    key: 'id',\n    color: 'cylinders',\n  }),\n]\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction voronoi<TDatum>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: VoronoiOptions<TDatum>,\n): ChartMark<never, never, never, InferredX, InferredY>\n```\n\nThe mark contributes x/y and color domains but intentionally emits no\n`ChartPoint` interaction candidates.\n\n## Options\n\n| Option                                            | Type                                      | Default          | Meaning                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `x`                                               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`            | Required         | Source horizontal position                            |\n| `y`                                               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`            | Required         | Source vertical position                              |\n| `z`                                               | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`              | One tessellation | Independent full-plot topology group                  |\n| `key`                                             | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`               | Inferred         | Stable source identity and coincident-cell ownership  |\n| `color`                                           | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`              | No value         | Value sent to the color scale; never a topology group |\n| `fill`                                            | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`           | Resolved color   | Cell fill                                             |\n| `stroke`                                          | `VisualChannel<TDatum, string>`           | None             | Cell stroke                                           |\n| `fillOpacity`, `strokeOpacity`, and `strokeWidth` | `number`                                  | Renderer default | Cell presentation                                     |\n| `strokeDasharray`                                 | `string`                                  | None             | Cell stroke dash pattern                              |\n| `opacity`                                         | `number`                                  | Renderer default | Whole-cell opacity                                    |\n| `id`                                              | `string`                                  | Layer-derived    | Stable mark ID                                        |\n| `motion`                                          | `ChartMarkMotionOptions<never>['motion']` | None             | Cell enter, update, and exit motion                   |\n\n`fill` and `stroke` accessors receive the original source row, source index,\nand complete source array.\n\n## Final-screen topology\n\nComplete x/y pairs establish the positional domains. After scales and inner\nbounds resolve, the mark projects both axes, computes the Voronoi diagram in\nscreen space, and clips every cell to the plot rectangle. Resizing can change\ncell geometry even when source values and domains are unchanged.\n\nInversion is not required, so continuous, temporal, and categorical\npositional scales are supported. Missing, nonfinite, or unmappable pairs are\nomitted without allowing one coordinate to expand the other coordinate's\ndomain. Zero valid positions produce no cells; one unique position fills the\nplot; two positions split it; and collinear positions remain valid.\n\nThe generated polygons are ordinary scene areas, not SVG path strings, so the\nsame definition works with SVG, Canvas, and static rendering.\n\n## Groups and identity\n\nAn explicit `z` creates an independent full-plot tessellation for every group.\nThose diagrams overlap. Omit `z` when all observations should share one\npartition; use `color` alone to paint categories without changing topology.\n\nSupply `key` when source order can change. Exact coincident positions produce\none cell within each `z` group, with ownership chosen from a canonical ordering\nof stable keys. This makes coincident ownership and cell keys deterministic\nacross reorderings. Distinct projected positions remain distinct even when\nthey are much closer than one pixel because nearby sites can still divide a\nlarge visible region. The mark fails instead of merging sites if separate cell\nboundaries cannot be represented by finite screen coordinates. Without an\nexplicit key, the mark tries a unique top-level or nested `id`, then falls back\nto source index.\n\n## Visible cells versus focus\n\nVoronoi polygons are a visible encoding layer, not an interaction index. They\nare hidden from accessibility navigation and add no pointer or keyboard focus\ncandidates. Layer `dot` or another interactive mark over the cells when the\nsource observations should drive nearest-point focus and tooltips. Both layers\ncan share x, y, color, and key channels without application-owned scale\nprojection or tooltip state.\n\nIf no cells should be painted and only lookup performance matters, provide a\n`ChartSpatialIndexFactory` instead of adding a `voronoi` mark. See\n[Tooltips and Focus](../../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md).\n"}
